why a true zero matters;
how to calculate all major columns of a frequency table;
why cumulative values are useful;
when grouped data are preferable to ungrouped data;
what is lost when data are grouped;
why apparent and real limits differ;
why bar-chart bars are separated while histogram bars touch;
how graphical scaling can mislead;
why the mean, median, and mode summarize different aspects of a distribution;
why the Week 2 sources disagree about rating-scale/Likert-like measurement.

Frequency Distributions
A frequency distribution compresses raw observations into an organized table.
The key columns are:

(f): frequency;
proportion: (f/N);
percentage: ((f/N));
CF: cumulative frequency;
cumulative percentage or cumulative relative frequency.

Think of cumulative frequency as:

“How many observations are at this value or less?”

The final cumulative frequency must equal (N), and the final cumulative percentage must equal 100%.

Apparent and Real Limits
A displayed whole-number score such as 21 has:

apparent value = 21;
lower real limit = 20.5;
upper real limit = 21.5.

This becomes important for grouped distributions and percentile calculations later.

