Study Guide

Week 03 §36 — What Should Be Reported Together?
Week 03 §38 — How the Concepts Fit Together

1. Why Learn Data Analysis?
Statistics is introduced in this course as a tool for making sense of observations. In psychology, good questions and good research designs are not enough by themselves. Researchers also need appropriate methods for summarizing and interpreting the data they collect.
A statistical procedure can help researchers:

organize observations;
summarize patterns;
compare groups;
examine relationships;
evaluate whether an observed pattern is consistent with a research hypothesis;
communicate evidence clearly.

The lecture emphasizes that statistics should help us move beyond intuition or “common sense.” Common sense can be incomplete, biased, or misleading. Statistical reasoning gives us a systematic way to evaluate claims.
At the same time, statistics does not automatically produce truth. Numbers, graphs, and analyses always appear in a context. Choices about what to measure, whom to sample, what comparison to make, and how to display results all affect the story that the analysis tells.

Key idea

Statistics is not just calculation. It is part of the broader process of asking questions, collecting evidence, evaluating claims, and communicating conclusions.

2. The Goals of Science
The lecture frames science as a search for regularities in the world.
Scientists may try to:

describe what happens;
identify reliable co-occurrences or laws;
search for causes;
develop theories that explain observed regularities.

A useful scientific theory must be falsifiable. This means that there must be some possible observation that could count against it.
Theories help researchers organize existing knowledge, predict new findings, and guide future research. Statistics enters this process by helping researchers determine whether patterns observed in data provide reasonable support for a proposed explanation.

3. From a Question to Data
A research project usually begins with something that needs explaining.
A researcher might:

observe something in the world;
read previous research;
talk with colleagues;
formulate a question;
decide what needs to be measured;
collect data;
analyze the data;
evaluate whether the evidence supports the original expectation.

The important transition is from an abstract idea to something measurable.

Operational definitions
Psychological concepts are often abstract. Anxiety, memory, stress, motivation, intelligence, and attention are not directly visible in the same way as height or weight.
Researchers therefore need to operationally define variables.
An operational definition states exactly how a concept will be observed or measured.
For example:

“anxiety” might be measured using a questionnaire score;
“memory” might be measured as the number of words correctly recalled;
“autonomic arousal” might be measured using heart rate or blood pressure.

Operational definitions connect theoretical ideas to observable data.

21. Tutorial Preparation
A TA should be ready to help students with both the conceptual language and the notation.

Conceptual questions students may ask

“Why do we need statistics if we can just look at the data?”
Raw data often contain too many individual observations to interpret easily. Descriptive statistics organize the observations; inferential statistics help researchers reason beyond the observed sample.


“Why can experiments show causation but correlations cannot?”
A properly conducted experiment uses manipulation, random assignment, and comparison/control. These design features help rule out competing explanations. A correlation only observes how variables co-vary.


“Why do we care so much about populations if we only collect samples?”
Psychological research usually aims to understand a broader group or process, not only the particular participants who happened to take part.


“Why is summation notation necessary?”
Statistical formulas often apply the same arithmetic operation to many observations. Summation notation provides a compact and precise way to express this.



Computation issues to watch for
Students often make mistakes by:

ignoring the lower and upper index of a summation;
confusing \(\sum X^2\) with \((\sum X)^2\);
multiplying \(\sum X\) and \(\sum Y\) when the expression actually requires \(\sum XY\);
forgetting that a constant inside a summation is applied to every included score;
skipping parentheses;
calculating cumulative frequencies incorrectly;
forgetting that proportions must sum to 1.00;
forgetting that percentages must sum to 100%;
drawing histogram bars with heights that do not match the frequency table.

23. What You Should Be Able to Explain
Before moving on, you should be able to explain—without relying on memorized definitions:

why statistics is part of scientific reasoning;
