46. Week 3 Takeaway
Week 3 adds two essential dimensions to descriptive statistics:

Where is the distribution centered, and how much do observations vary around that center?

A single average is never the whole story.
To describe data responsibly, consider:

the appropriate measure of center;
the shape of the distribution;
the presence of outliers;
the amount of variability;
whether the data are a sample or a population.

The mean and standard deviation will become especially important in later weeks, but the deeper lesson is broader:

Good statistical description requires understanding both location and spread.

Median and Percentile Interpolation
The official formula sheet provides:
[ Md = LL + W . ]
The general percentile formula is:
[ P_x = LL + W . ]
Know what each symbol means:

(LL): lower real limit of the target interval;
(W): interval width;
(n): total sample size;
(cf_{}): cumulative frequency below the target interval;
(f): frequency within the target interval.

For this course, frequency tables used for these calculations are arranged in descending order, so the cumulative frequency below the target row represents smaller scores.

Transformations
If a constant (C) is added to every score:

mean increases by (C);
variance is unchanged;
SD is unchanged.

If every score is multiplied by (C):

mean is multiplied by (C);
SD is multiplied by (|C|);
variance is multiplied by (C^2).

